<blockquote class="uncited"><div>How do you formulate the constraint?</div></blockquote>Sorry I replied to myself without refreshing the page,<br>The constraints are the same used by Kenny in his thesis.<br>The difference will come from the way we calculate the error (both angular and linear).<br><br>For the rotations, I'm using the rotation matrix to  go from the base of object 1 to the one of object 2. From that I'm getting the sin(phi).v which I approximate to phi.v. I'm using the local axis and local version of the two orthogonals to compute the base.<br><br>For the linear error, I save the initial distance between the two gravity centers projected on the two orthogonals, and I calculate them back at each iteration.<br><br>Thanks<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=728">gee</a> — Thu Aug 24, 2006 9:39 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
